Is a large population a problem or a power? The honest answer: **it depends on its quality**.

A big population means more mouths to feed, more pressure on land, water, forests, housing and transport. Crowded cities face slums, traffic and pollution. If people are unhealthy, unschooled and unskilled, numbers become a **burden**.

But people are also producers. A healthy, educated, skilled population — **human capital** — builds roads, writes software, runs hospitals and farms better. Japan, with few natural resources, became rich largely by investing in its people.

India today has an unusually large share of people in the **working-age group (15–59)**. When the working-age share is high and the dependent share low, a country can enjoy a **demographic dividend** — more earners per dependant, more savings, faster growth. But the dividend is not automatic. It pays out only if young people get:
- good **schooling** and **skills** matched to jobs;
- **health and nutrition** (an anaemic teenager cannot study or work at full strength);
- **jobs** — enough of them, in industry and services;
- **equal opportunity for women**, who are half the potential workforce.

If these fail, the same bulge of young people can mean unemployment and frustration. That is why the National Population Policy focuses not just on numbers but on schooling, health and the status of girls.

> *Did you know?* India's population is also slowly **ageing**: the share of people above 60 is rising, so pensions, elder care and health services for seniors will matter more each decade.

Recognising that planned families improve individual health and welfare, the Government of India began a comprehensive **Family Planning Programme in 1952**. The Family Welfare Programme promotes **responsible and planned parenthood on a voluntary basis**.

The **National Population Policy (NPP) 2000** provides a framework for:
- **free and compulsory school education up to 14 years** of age;
- **reducing the infant mortality rate to below 30 per 1,000** live births;
- **universal immunisation** of children against all vaccine-preventable diseases;
- promoting **delayed marriage for girls**;
- making **family welfare a people-centred programme**.

**NPP 2000 and adolescents.** The policy identified adolescents as a group needing special attention. Besides nutrition, it emphasised protection from unwanted pregnancies and sexually transmitted diseases, encouraging delayed marriage and child-bearing, education about health risks, accessible and affordable health services, food supplements and nutritional services, and stronger legal measures to **prevent child marriage**.

**People as resource.** A country's most valuable resource is its people. A healthy, educated, skilled population — **human capital** — can turn a large population into a strength. That is the theme of the Economics chapter 'People as Resource'.

> *Did you know?* India's **total fertility rate** (average children per woman) fell to about **2.0** in the National Family Health Survey 2019–21 — just below the replacement level of 2.1. Population will still grow for some decades because so many people are young — this is called *population momentum*.

**Zimbabwe.** Zimbabwe attained independence from white minority rule in **1980**. Since then the country has been ruled by **ZANU-PF**, the party that led the freedom struggle. Its leader, **Robert Mugabe**, ruled the country from independence (till 2017). Elections were held regularly and always won by ZANU-PF. President Mugabe was popular but also used **unfair practices** in elections. Over the years his government **changed the Constitution several times** to increase the powers of the President and make him less accountable. Opposition party workers were **harassed** and their meetings disrupted. Public protests and demonstrations against the government were declared illegal. There was a law that limited the right to criticise the President. Television and radio were controlled by the government and gave only the ruling party's version. There were independent newspapers but the government harassed those journalists who went against it. The government **ignored some court judgments** that went against it and pressurised judges.

So Zimbabwe had elections, a popular leader and majority support — yet it was not a democracy in the full sense. Why? Because:
- citizens could not freely criticise the government;
- the opposition could not organise freely;
- the media was not free;
- courts were not respected.

> **Feature 4:** A democratic government rules **within limits set by constitutional law and citizens' rights**. Popular governments can be undemocratic. Popular leaders can be autocratic. A democracy must be based on **free and fair elections** — and must also respect **basic rights** (freedom of thought, expression, association, protest) and the **rule of law** (independent courts, equality before law).

**Summary definition.** Putting it all together, democracy is **a form of government in which**:
1. rulers elected by the people take all the **major decisions**;
2. elections offer a **choice and fair opportunity** to the people to change the current rulers;
3. this choice and opportunity is available to **all the people on an equal basis**; and
4. the exercise of this choice leads to a government **limited by basic rules of the constitution and citizens' rights**.
